  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › How to learn Spanish</p>
  <h1>How to learn Spanish: a beginner plan</h1>
  <p class="lede">Spanish is one of the most learner-friendly languages in the world: its spelling matches its sounds almost perfectly, its pronunciation is regular, and it is spoken by hundreds of millions of people. Start with sounds and core words, then verbs, then speaking every day.</p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn Spanish pronunciation (it is easy)</h2>
  <p>Spanish has five clear vowel sounds — a, e, i, o, u — and each letter almost always makes the same sound. Once you learn the sounds, you can read any Spanish word out loud. Learn the few tricky letters early: the rolled “r”, the soft “c” and “z”, and the silent “h”.</p>
  <p>Practise reading aloud from day one. Because Spanish spelling is so regular, reading aloud is one of the best ways to train your mouth and your ear at the same time.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn core words and gender</h2>
  <p>Like French, every Spanish noun is masculine or feminine — el libro, la mesa. Learn each noun with el or la, so the gender sticks. Start with greetings (hola, buenos días, gracias, por favor), numbers, time, food, family and everyday verbs like ser, estar, tener and ir.</p>
  <p>Spanish shares thousands of words with English — nación/nation, teléfono/telephone, idea/idea. These cognates give you a large vocabulary almost for free.</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Learn ser and estar, the two verbs for “to be”</h2>
  <p>Spanish has two verbs where English has one. Ser is for things that are lasting or define something: “Soy estudiante” (I am a student). Estar is for temporary states and locations: “Estoy cansado” (I am tired), “Estoy en casa” (I am at home).</p>
  <p>This confuses every beginner, and it is completely normal. Learn the basic rule, then notice the difference in real sentences — it becomes natural with practice, not with memorising rules.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Learn the present tense</h2>
  <p>Spanish verbs change endings for each person, and there are three regular groups: -ar (hablar), -er (comer) and -ir (vivir). Learn the endings for one group well, and the other two will feel familiar. Add the most common irregular verbs — ser, estar, ir, tener — early, because you will use them constantly.</p>
  <p>You do not need to learn every tense at once. The present tense plus a few time words (hoy, mañana, ayer) already lets you talk about your day, your plans and your past.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Listen and speak every day</h2>
  <p>Spanish is spoken in many countries, and accents differ — but the core language is the same. Listen to music, shows and podcasts in Spanish, and read short texts. Try to understand the main idea first, not every word.</p>
  <p>Speak from week one: introduce yourself, order food, ask simple questions. A tutor helps you practise real conversations and corrects the small mistakes that books cannot catch.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Confusing ser and estar — learn the basic rule, then learn from examples.</li>
    <li>Translating English phrases word for word, especially “I am” sentences.</li>
    <li>Worrying about rolling your “r” perfectly before you speak at all.</li>
    <li>Learning long vocabulary lists without ever using the words in sentences.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Read aloud every day — Spanish spelling makes this the easiest win in any language.</li>
    <li>Learn nouns with el or la from the start.</li>
    <li>Learn 5–10 words a day inside sentences, not alone.</li>
    <li>Listen to one Spanish song a day and learn its chorus.</li>
  </ul>
