  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn Spanish?</summary><p>Spanish is one of the fastest languages for English speakers to learn. With 30 minutes a day, most learners can hold simple conversations in 2–4 months and reach a comfortable everyday level within a year. Regular pronunciation and thousands of shared words make the start much easier than most languages.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is Spanish hard to learn?</summary><p>The beginning is easy — regular spelling and pronunciation. The middle gets harder: verbs change a lot, and ser/estar and por/para take time. But Spanish grammar is regular and logical, and there is a huge amount of music, TV and books to practise with.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Should I learn European or Latin American Spanish?</summary><p>Start with either — the core language is the same, and you will be understood everywhere. The main differences are a few sounds, some words, and the use of “vosotros” in Spain. Pick the variety that matches your reason for learning: travel, family, work or media.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn Spanish by myself?</summary><p>Yes, a long way — especially reading, listening and grammar. Speaking is the part that needs a partner, and a tutor is the fastest way to get real conversation practice with corrections.</p></details>
